INDEX -- what is on this disk, and where it came from======================================================Every command here was tested with THIS DISK AS THE ONLY DISK (os9exec/dd/CMDS/sh), no Microware module present anywhere -- no shell, csl, cio ormath.  Note this disk does not "boot": os9exec is the kernel and simply runsa program with this disk mounted as /dd.  *  A STAR before a name means that program needs Microware's trap-handler     modules (cio, and for some csl and math).  It will NOT run on this disk     by itself: it stops with "**** Can't install trap handler ****".  Supply     your own cio and it works -- see DOC/README-CIO for how to set that up.     92 programs are starred: 79 in CMDS, 7 in CMDS/GAMES, 5 in     CMDS/REBUILT and 1 in CMDS/BROKEN.  Everything unstarred ran     standalone.These are not broken and they are not going anywhere.  They were compiled theordinary way for the era (cc -qixm), which is a licensing question, not aquality one.Provenance: everything came from the Microware OS-9 hobbyist archive, theHamburg OS-9 Users Group, and other public preservation sites. The originalarchives are kept in CMDS/archives/ -- ed, elvis and grep include full Csource in there. Per-package documentation is in DOC/ (see DOC/README).SOURCES.txt carries fuller per-command provenance and licences.Five commands here are OUR OWN BUILDS, not archive binaries: cat, wc, basenameand dirname were rebuilt from source with the gcc2 toolchain against os9lib,because every archived build of them needs cio; and ls is our repaired buildof GNU fileutils 3.13. Their C source is on the h0 workshop disk.------------------------------------------------------/dd/CMDS -- WORKS ON THIS DISK (80 commands)------------------------------------------------------  ar             archive librarian (Carl Kreider) -- .ar files  as0            68000 assembler (xasm)  as1            68010 assembler (xasm)  as11           68HC11 assembler (xasm)  a