art of Microware's OS-9 SDK.  They are not in this collection -- if you runOS-9 you have them already, and DOC/README-CIO shows how to point at them.DOC/README-CIO explains the three ways to set this up.All 92, verified by running every program on this disk with no cio present:  aprocs        as09          autolf        back          basename.cio  bm            cal           cat.cio       cb            chbase  checkenv      chgrp         chown         ci            ckermit  co            cp            cribbage      cxref         delbak  detab         dir           dirname.cio   divide        dm  dmake         dpark         ed            edir          emacs  emacs.mm1     ephem         ephem881      eset          eunlink  exist         fc            fgrep         field         forth  gdd           getenv        gnuchess      gnuchessn     gnuchessr  hotel         initvdu       ksh           l             larn  launch        loglist       mexist        mg            mille  mkdir         move          mv            owner         paranoia  perr          piano         printenv      psc           qsort9  rcs           rcsdiff       rcsident      rcsmerge      rechne  rlog          rm            robots        setimex       setterm  shdev         signal        strings.cio   sysmon        tail  todos         toos9         trap          uniq          unzip  uudecode      uuencode      vi_cio        wc.cio        wysecrack  xkermit       zoo------------------------------------------------------What is here, and what is not------------------------------------------------------A /dd/CMDS/NONFREE directory used to hold six programs recorded as SDKbinaries.  Four of those records turned out to be mistaken, and the two thatwere right belong with the SDK rather than here:  ar     Carl Kreider's archiver        -> /dd/CMDS/ar  ctags  BSD ctags (UC Regents)         -> /dd/CMDS/ctags  VI     PVIC, public domain            -> /dd/CMDS/REBUILT (name wa